he selected deployment topology.\n53\t- A getting-started walkthrough: connecting to the feature store from a\n54\t  supported workbench with the preinstalled Feast SDK, including pointing at\n55\t  customer-managed storage for the offline store.\n56\t- A supported-limits topic: what is supported and what is not — notably that\n57\t  offline-store infrastructure is customer-provided (managed offline-store\n58\t  infrastructure is out of scope for this release) and that the Dashboard\n59\t  integration is link-out only (no feature-authoring UI).\n60\t- Screenshots/references for the Dashboard component presence and endpoint\n61\t  links, captured from the implemented behavior.\n62\t\n63\t## Acceptance Criteria\n64\t\n65\t- Published documentation covers enablement, getting started, and supported\n66\t  limits as separate topics addressing HLR-4's three areas.\n67\t- The getting-started walkthrough is technically accurate against the\n68\t  delivered component: the enablement steps, workbench SDK usage, and\n69\t  customer-managed offline-store configuration all work as documented.\n70\t- The supported-limits topic explicitly states the out-of-scope boundaries\n71\t  (no managed offline-store infrastructure; Dashboard link-out only).\n72\t- The documentation is technically reviewed against the implementation by the\n73\t  owning teams before publication.\n74\t\n75\t## HLR Traceability\n76\t\n77\t- HLR-4 (P2): \"Product documentation covers enablement, a getting-started\n78\t  walkthrough, and supported limits.\"\n79\t\n80\t## Dependencies and Constraints\n81\t\n82\t- Blocked by all implementation work in this strategy: the midstream\n83\t  repository and build pipeline (what is shipped), the license outcome (what\n84\t  can be shipped), the workbench SDK image (the getting-started surface), the\n85\t  Dashboard integration (what the screenshots show), and the deployment\n86\t  topology decision (what the enablement steps say).\n87\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-